# Food (talk.food, doc 62 phase 7): meals and cooking, the realm's dishes, feast and famine, the tavern's cook, and
# the wolf's honest opinion of cooked meat. Broad scenes first (any two residents), then by season, place, trade,
# region and standing. Parallel alternatives answer each other by position; tagged lines carry the thread.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Anyone, any day

scene e1_food_burnt
when topic=food
a : I burnt the #supper# again. | Smoke everywhere. I'll be eating cold bread tonight. | Do you know a way to get black off a pot?
b[cook] : Lid on, fire low, and stay in the room. That's the whole secret.
b[spouses] : I know. I was there. I'm still coughing.
b : Again? What was it this time? | Better than burnt bread. | Sand and patience.
a : Porridge. Who burns porridge? Me, apparently. | Only just. | I've neither.
b?[humour=merry] : Call it smoked. Smoked porridge. Charge a penny for it.

scene e1_food_nose_and_tongue
when topic=food
a : Smell that? Somebody's frying onions. | My nose has been following that smell since the corner. | Whoever's cooking with that much garlic owes the street an apology.
b[young] : My nose is better than yours. It's two doors down. The blue door.
b : Onions and something else. Liver? | Mine too. Let's knock. | I like garlic.
a : Liver. Gods, I hope it's liver. | Can't knock on a stranger's door for liver. | You would.
b : Shame we weren't asked. | You could. I couldn't. | Somebody has to.

scene e1_food_raw_honest
when topic=food
a : Do you ever think cooking meat is a waste of good meat? | Don't tell anyone, but I ate my mutton half raw last night. | Sometimes I just want to bite something. You know?
b[old] : My grandfather never cooked a thing in his life. Teeth like a trap, till ninety.
b[healer] : Cook it through. I've seen what raw pork does to a belly.
b[priest] : We are not the wild ones. That's the whole point of the fire.
b : Every day. We're civilised, apparently. | Half raw's just right. | I know exactly what you mean.
a : Civilised and hungry. | Shh. Not a word to my mother. | Don't look at me like that, then.

scene e1_food_fur_in_soup
when topic=food
a : Found fur in my soup at the tavern. Not mine. | The cook's shedding. It's in everything. | Spring's the worst. You get a mouthful of somebody with every stew.
b[cook] : Everyone's shedding. I wear a cap. What more do you want, a sack?
b : Whose was it? | Pick it out and carry on. | Comb before you cook, I say.
a : Grey. I've my suspicions. | That's what I did. | Tell the cook, not me.

scene e1_food_last_crust
when topic=food
a : Who gets the last piece, in your house? | My brothers fight for the crust like it's gold. | Never leave the last slice. My mother says it's bad luck.
b[young] : Me. I'm the smallest. Smallest gets it. That's the rule.
b[old] : The eldest. And I'm the eldest.
b : Whoever's quickest. | It is gold. Best bit. | Mine says it's greed.
a : Then it's never me. | I let them. I'm a saint. | Same thing, really.

scene e1_food_secret_ingredient
when topic=food
a : My stew's got a secret in it. | I put a spoon of honey in the gravy. Don't laugh. | There's one thing my mother put in her pies and she took it to her grave.
b[cook] : Everyone's secret is salt. They just don't know it.
b[honesty=sly] : I'll find out. I always find out.
b : Go on, tell me. | That's not daft. That's clever. | Probably lard.
a : Then it'd not be a secret. | It is, isn't it? | Probably. It was always lard.

scene e1_food_eating_alone
when topic=food
a : I hate eating on my own. | Do you ever just eat standing over the pot? | I've eaten every meal this week by the window, watching the street.
b[widowed=yes] : Every night. You lay the table anyway. It helps, a little.
b[spouses] : You've never once eaten alone. I'm always there.
b : Come and eat with us, then. | Every night. Saves a bowl. | There's worse company than a street.
a : I might. | Saves washing. | It's better than the wall.

scene e1_food_too_full
when topic=food
a : I ate too much. I'm going to lie down in the road. | Never let me near a pie again. | Third bowl. I'm not proud.
b[cook] : That's the best thing anyone's said to me all week.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : I'd like to have that trouble.
b : Get up, you'll be run over. | I'll hold you to that. | You should be.
a : Leave me. I'm happy. | You won't. | I'm a little proud.

scene e1_food_swap
when topic=food
a : Swap you a jar of plums for a bit of that cheese. | I've more eggs than I can eat. Want some? | I'll trade you bread for a fish, if you've one going.
b[farmer] : Eggs? I've hens. Bring me salt and we'll talk.
b[fisher] : I've always a fish going. You'll want it gutted?
b : Done. | I'll never say no to eggs. | Fair trade.
a : Bring the cheese round, then. | Half a dozen, then. | Good.

scene e1_food_mother_said
when topic=food
a : My mother used to say a meal without bread isn't a meal. | My father ate his supper in four bites, every night of his life. | My grandmother made us chew everything twenty times.
b[old] : They were right, mostly. Mothers usually are.
b : She'd hate what I eat now, then. | Wolves of that age ate like that. | Did you?
a : She'd weep. | Like a trap closing. | Never. Not once.

scene e1_food_feeding_neighbours
when topic=food
a : I made too much soup. Want a bowl? | There's half a pie going spare at mine. | Someone left a loaf on my doorstep. No note.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : Gods bless you. Yes. Yes, please.
b[warmth=cold] : No, thank you.
b : I'll bring my own bowl. | I'll take it off your paws. | Was it warm?
a : Bring a big one. | Gladly. | Still warm. Somebody's kind.

scene e1_food_pickling
when topic=food here.season=summer|autumn
a : I'm pickling everything that stands still. | The whole house smells of vinegar. | Got forty jars of onions down the cellar.
b[old] : Good. You'll be glad of it in the hungry months.
b : Not the cat, I hope. | Better than damp. | Forty!
a : The cat's quick. | Marginally. | I'll be sick of onions by midwinter.

scene e1_food_hungry_gap
when topic=food here.season=spring
a : The cellar's near empty and nothing's up yet. | Nettle soup again. | It's the hungry gap. Everything's dear and nothing's grown.
b[farmer] : A few more weeks. The greens are coming. I can see them, just.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : Every spring the same. You'd think we'd learn.
b : We'll manage. | Nettle soup's not so bad. | It's always the worst bit of the year.
a : We always do. Just. | It's not so good either. | Roll on summer.

scene e1_food_cold_supper
when topic=food here.season=summer
a : Too hot to cook. Bread and cheese again. | I'm living on cucumbers. | Nobody should light an oven in this weather.
b[baker] : I light mine every morning. You get used to it. You don't.
b : Bread and cheese is a feast. | Cucumbers aren't food. | Tell that to the baker.
a : Cold ale with it. | They're water you chew. | Poor baker.

scene e1_food_winter_stew
when topic=food here.season=winter
a : A pot of stew that's been on the fire all day. That's winter done right. | My stew's been on since Restday. It's still going. | Nothing warms like broth on a #cold# night.
b[old] : We kept one going all winter when I was a pup. Just added to it.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : Water and bones, mine. Still warm, though.
b : Save me a bowl. | Since Restday? Is it safe? | Broth and a blanket.
a : Bring bread. | Safer every day. It's practically armour now. | And a fire that doesn't smoke.

scene e1_food_apples
when topic=food here.season=autumn
a : Apples in everything this time of year. | I've apples coming out of my ears. | Somebody's tree is dropping apples all over the lane.
b[child] : I took some. They were on the ground! That's not stealing.
b : I'm not complaining. | Make a cake. | Shame to waste them.
a : Nor me. Apple and pork. | I've made three. | I've a sack, if you've a mind.

scene e1_food_feast_hunger
when topic=food here.day=festival
a : Eat everything. Tomorrow it's bread again. | I've had four pies and I mean to have a fifth. | Look at all that food. Where's it all been hiding?
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : I'm filling my pockets. I'm not ashamed.
b[wealth=rich] : Rather common fare, isn't it? Still. I'll have a pie.
b : I mean to. | Pace yourself. | The cellars of the rich.
a : Starting now. | I'll pace myself tomorrow. | Good of them to share.

scene e1_food_restday_roast
when topic=food here.day=rest
a : Restday roast. The one good meal of the week. | Something's roasting in every house in the street. | I'm saving myself for the Restday dinner.
b[piety=devout] : After chapel, mind. Not before.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : A roast. What's that like, then?
b : The one thing worth waiting for. | Smells like it. | Don't fill up on bread.
a : Every week I wait. | I can hardly stand it. | Wouldn't dream of it.

scene e1_food_market_day
when topic=food here.day=market
a : I came for flour and I'm going home with a whole goose. | Market days I eat my way round the stalls. | Did you see the size of those cabbages?
b[merchant] : That's how we like it. Come back next week.
b : That's how it goes. | Me too. Pie, then sausage, then more pie. | Big as a pup's head.
a : I still forgot the flour. | My purse is lighter and I'm happy. | Bigger.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- At the tavern, at home, at the table

scene e1_food_tavern_stew
when topic=food here.place=tavern
a : What's in the stew today? | Don't ask what's in the stew. | The stew here is the best thing in town and the worst thing in town, depending.
b[innkeeper] : Meat. Vegetable. Gravy. Ask me no more.
b : Yesterday's stew. | I didn't. | Depending on what?
a : And the day before's. | Wise. | On how much ale you've had.

scene e1_food_tavern_cook_temper
when topic=food here.place=tavern
a : The cook's in a temper. I heard a pan hit the wall. | Don't send anything back. The cook bites. | The cook's crying in the yard again.
b[cook] : I'd throw pans too, with what they pay.
b : Was it full? | I've heard that. | Onions, or feelings?
a : Of beans. | I'm eating it, whatever it is. | Both, I think.

scene e1_food_tavern_pie
when topic=food here.place=tavern here.time=dusk|night
a : One more pie and I'll walk home. | Their pies are better after dark. Don't ask me why. | I only came in for the pies.
b[humour=merry] : Everything's better after dark.
b : You'll roll home. | It's the ale. | Liar.
a : Rolling's walking, nearly. | It's the ale. | I came for the ale too.

scene e1_food_table_manners
when topic=food here.place=home|hall
a : Elbows off the table. | Who taught you to eat? Wild things? | Use the spoon. That's what it's for.
b[young] : The spoon's too small for my muzzle!
b[family] : You eat with your paws too. I've seen you.
b : Yes, mother. | My mother, actually. | It's slower.
a : Don't you yes-mother me. | She'd be ashamed. | It's meant to be.

scene e1_food_tablecloth
when topic=food here.place=home
a : We've a tablecloth now. A proper one. | Don't spill on that. It was my mother's. | I've laid the table nicely for once.
b[wealth=rich] : We've had tablecloths for years. Linen ones.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : A cloth? On the table? Whatever for?
b : Very grand. | I'll try not to. | Who's coming?
a : I'm told we're coming up in the world. | Try very hard. | Nobody. It's for me.

scene e1_food_kitchen_help
when topic=food here.place=home|work
a : Chop these, would you? | Stir that and don't stop. | Watch the pot while I fetch water.
b[young] : Can I lick the spoon after?
b[cook] : Give me that. You're doing it wrong.
b : Thick or thin? | How long? | It's not going anywhere.
a : Thin. Like your patience. | Till I say. | That's what you think.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Drink

scene e1_food_ale_or_cider
when topic=food
a : Ale or cider, if there were only one? | The new barrel at the tavern's sour. | I've given up ale. It's been two days.
b[brewer] : Ale. And it isn't sour, it's sharp. There's a difference.
b[from=southmeadow] : Cider. Or wine. Wine, really.
b : Ale, and I'll fight you. | Sour as a priest. | Two days! A record.
a : Cider, and I'll win. | Sour as a priest's wife. | Three tomorrow. Maybe.

scene e1_food_milk
when topic=food
a : Bought a jug of milk and it turned by noon. | Is there anything better than fresh milk? | Warm milk before bed. My one weakness.
b[herder] : Keep it in the cold water. Not on the windowsill.
b : Never on the windowsill. | Cream. | Mine's cheese.
a : Now you tell me. | Cream, then. | That's not a weakness. That's a character.

scene e1_food_tea
when topic=food
a : My mother brews mint for every ailment there is. | Nettle tea. It's free and it tastes it. | A hot cup of something, and I'm a new wolf.
b[healer] : She's not wrong. Mint for the belly, sage for the throat.
b : Mine was chamomile. | Honey helps. | Make me one, then.
a : Smells like a meadow. | Everything helps with honey. | Fetch the kettle.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Cooks, bakers, the trades of the table

scene e1_food_cook_feet
when topic=food a.job=cook
a : Twelve hours over the fire and I've not eaten a bite. | I can't taste anything any more. Everything's just salt or not salt. | They sent the plate back. The plate. Back.
b[cook] : I know. You eat standing, from the spoon, and it doesn't count.
b : Sit down. Eat something. | Is that bad? | What was wrong with it?
a : If I sit down, I'll not get up. | Ask the customers. | Nothing. They were wrong.

scene e1_food_baker_dawn
when topic=food a.job=baker
a : Up since the third hour. The loaves won't knead themselves. | The oven's playing up. Half the batch came out flat. | I dream about bread. It's not restful.
b[child] : Do you ever eat the bread? Or is it all for selling?
b : Smells worth it from here. | Flat bread's still bread. | What do you dream?
a : It is, every morning. | Not to my customers. | Dough. Rising. Rising forever.

scene e1_food_baker_eats
when topic=food b.job=baker
a : Is there a trick to a good crust? | How do you keep from eating the stock? | Why does your bread taste better than mine?
b : Steam and a hot oven. | I don't. Look at me. | A hundred thousand loaves. That's the trick.
a : Steam. I'll try. | You look fine. | I've made maybe forty.

scene e1_food_brewer_tasting
when topic=food a.job=brewer
a : I've to taste every barrel. It's a cruel trade. | This batch came out like river water. | Somebody's been at my best barrel. I know the level.
b[friends] : I'll help. For you. Out of friendship.
b : Cruel. Sure. | Sell it cheap. | Not me.
a : You're very kind. | That's the plan. | I'd know if it was you.

scene e1_food_fisher_eats
when topic=food a.job=fisher
a : I've eaten fish every day for twenty years and I'd still have it tonight. | Best bit of the fish? The cheek. Nobody knows. | We keep the little ones for ourselves. The big ones sell.
b[child] : I don't like fish. The eyes look at you.
b : Fish every day? | The cheek? | Little ones are sweeter.
a : Every day. | Tiny, sweet. Best bite going. | They are. Don't tell the buyers.

scene e1_food_farmer_eats
when topic=food a.job=farmer
a : Grow it all and still buy my bread in town. | My own peas. Nothing tastes like your own peas. | I'd have more to eat if I didn't have to sell it.
b[merchant] : And I'd have less to sell. That's the way of it.
b : That's farming for you. | I'll take your word. | Isn't that the way.
a : That's the joke of it. | Come by, I'll prove it. | It is.

scene e1_food_herder_mutton
when topic=food a.job=herder
a : People ask if I eat mutton. Of course I do. | Never name the lambs. I named one. | They think herders eat lamb every night. We eat porridge.
b : Isn't it hard, though? | What was its name? | Porridge?
a : Not when it's roasted. | Clover. She was lovely. | Lamb's for selling.

scene e1_food_guard_rations
when topic=food a.job=guard|soldier
a : The barracks porridge could hold a wall up. | Supper on the watch is a heel of bread and a cold sausage. | I can eat standing, walking or asleep.
b[guard] : You've not had the barracks pea soup, then. Count yourself lucky.
b : Well, it's walls you guard. | Still better than nothing. | All three at once?
a : Useful. | Barely. | Once. Nobody noticed.

scene e1_food_miner_pasty
when topic=food a.job=miner
a : A pasty you can hold in a dirty paw. That's a miner's supper. | Down there you'd eat the lamp oil if you were hungry enough. | You eat the crimped edge last. Or you leave it for the spirits.
b[from=frosthollow] : Leave it for the spirits. Always. My father did.
b : Better than a spoon. | You're not serious. | Is that true?
a : Better than most things. | Nearly. | True as rock.

scene e1_food_priest_feast
when topic=food a.job=priest
a : Gluttony's a sin, they tell me. Pass the potatoes. | I've three suppers to attend tonight. My flock feeds me well. | Grace before eating. Then eat quickly before it cools.
b[piety=devout] : The gods give food to be enjoyed. In measure.
b : I won't tell. | Three! | That's my sort of prayer.
a : In measure. Pass the gravy too. | It's a burden I bear. | The gods understand.

scene e1_food_healer_advice
when topic=food b.job=healer
a : What's good for a sour belly? | I've been eating nothing but bread. Is that bad? | Does garlic really keep the ill humours off?
b : Ginger. Or less ale. | Add some greens, or you'll be in my chair. | It keeps everyone off.
a : Less ale. Hm. | I'd rather not. | Good enough.

scene e1_food_servant_scraps
when topic=food a.job=servant
a : The family had seven dishes last night. We had what came back. | They waste more than we eat. | I carry the plates past my own nose. It's a kind of torture.
b[servant] : The best bits come back. They never eat the crackling.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : What came back? Bring some home.
b : Seven! | Shame. | Don't drop them.
a : Seven. Two were jellies. | It is a shame. | I want to.

scene e1_food_child_sweets
when topic=food a.stage=young
a : I found a honeycomb. I'm not sharing. | Did you know you can eat clover? | I'm never eating cabbage again. Never.
b[old] : When I was your age we'd have been glad of cabbage.
b[young] : Give me some or I'll tell.
b : Not even a bit? | The flowers? | Not even once?
a : Not even a bit. | The flowers. They're sweet. | Never ever.

scene e1_food_old_teeth
when topic=food a.stage=old
a : I can't chew the crusts any more. Soak everything in milk. | My teeth went before my appetite. Cruel. | Soup, soup, soup. I'd sell my soul for a chop.
b[young] : You can have my crusts! I hate crusts.
b[old] : Same as me. I soak my bread in the ale. Kills two birds.
b : Soft bread, then. | Cruel indeed. | I'll chop it small for you.
a : Soft as a bed. | Very cruel. | Small as you like. I'll suck it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Wealth and want

scene e1_food_rich_dinner
when topic=food a.wealth=rich
a : We had a fish at dinner as long as my arm. | Our cook's done a dish with saffron. I can't say I liked it. | Twelve to dinner tomorrow. Twelve!
b[wealth=poor|destitute] #envy : I had a turnip.
b[wealth=rich] : Saffron's overrated. I said it at the Merriots' and was never asked again.
b : Lovely. | What's it taste of? | Who's the twelve?
a@envy[warmth=warm] : Come to dinner, then. I mean it. I'll set a place.
a : Delicious. | Like money. | Nobody I like.

scene e1_food_poor_bread
when topic=food a.wealth=poor|destitute
a : Bread and dripping, again. | I've a crust. I'll make it last till evening. | The parish loaf's bigger than the bakers' now.
b[wealth=rich] : Oh, dripping. How hearty.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : Dripping's a luxury, at mine.
b : Dripping's not bad. | Make it last. | It's the bread of shame, that.
a : Not bad, every day? It is. | I always do. | Shame fills a belly.

scene e1_food_pawned_pot
when topic=food a.wealth=poor|destitute
a : I sold my best pot. Now I've nothing to cook in. | We eat once a day now. Supper. Makes it something to look forward to. | Do you know who gives out soup? I've heard there's someone.
b[warmth=warm] : You'll come to mine tonight. Don't argue.
b[piety=devout] : The chapel gives soup on Restday. Go early.
b : That's a hard thing. | That's one way of seeing it. | The chapel, I think.
a : I'll get it back. | It's the only way. | I'll try there.

scene e1_food_hungry_now
when topic=food a.hungry=yes
a : I'm #hungry#. Talk about something else. | Don't mention food. I mean it. | My belly's growling like a dog.
b[cook] : Come round the back of the kitchen. Quietly.
b : What, like pie? | Roast pork. | I can hear it from here.
a : I hate you. | Stop. | It's growled since noon.

scene e1_food_dole_bread
when topic=food a.on_dole=yes
a : The dole loaf's grey this week. | I queued so long for the parish bread I could've baked it. | They gave me a loaf and a sermon.
b[priest] : The sermon's free. The loaf isn't.
b : Grey bread's still bread. | They don't make it easy. | Which was better?
a : Grey's a colour, not a food. | They mean not to. | The loaf. Just.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ridgemere: harbour fare, Heights and Sump

scene e1_food_rm_mussels
when topic=food here.region=ridgemere
a : Fresh mussels off the wharf. A bucket for two pennies. | Herring again. This city floats on herring. | Have you had the crab from the Harbour Hill stall?
b[from=larkfield] : I'll never get used to eating things with shells.
b : A bucket! | Swims on it, more like. | Not with my purse.
a : A whole bucket. | Swims. | Worth saving for.

scene e1_food_rm_fog_chowder
when topic=food here.region=ridgemere here.weather=rain|fog|storm
cooldown 2d
a : A chowder on a day like this. That's the city's one mercy. | Rain outside, fish soup inside. Right as it should be. | The pie stall's got a queue round the corner in this #rain#.
b[fisher] : Chowder's what we do with the catch nobody buys.
b : Its only mercy. | That's Ridgemere. | Everyone's had the same idea.
a : I'll take the leftovers. | Wet in, wet out. | I'll queue.

scene e1_food_rm_heights_dinner
when topic=food here.region=ridgemere a.quarter=heights
a : Another dinner on the Heights. Eight courses. I'll burst. | They served larks' tongues. Larks' tongues! | Our cook won't speak to me since I asked for plain mutton.
b[quarter=sump] : Larks' tongues. I'd eat the lark whole and say thank you.
b[quarter=heights] : The Greatlys do a better table. Don't tell anyone I said so.
b : What a trial for you. | Who eats tongues? | Plain mutton's no crime.
a : It is a trial. | People with too much money. | She thinks it is.

scene e1_food_rm_sump_supper
when topic=food here.region=ridgemere a.quarter=sump
a : Down in the Sump, supper's whatever the tide brings. | Twenty of us share one hearth in our building. You cook in turns. | There's a fellow sells eel heads by the Sump steps. Cheap.
b[quarter=heights] : Goodness. Do you really?
b : The tide's generous, sometimes. | What's your turn? | Eel heads?
a : Really. | Third. After the shouting. | Boiled, they're not bad. Mostly.

scene e1_food_rm_wharf_pie
when topic=food here.region=ridgemere a.quarter=wharf|docks
a : The pie seller on the wharf's got fish in everything. Even the apple ones. | Dockers' breakfast: a pint and a herring. | They're unloading oranges. The whole quay smells of them.
b[sailor] : You've never had ship's biscuit. Be grateful.
b : Even the apple? | Breakfast of kings. | Southmeadow's?
a : Even the apple. | Kings with backache. | Smells like it.

scene e1_food_rm_smoke_works
when topic=food here.region=ridgemere a.quarter=works
a : Everything up at the Works tastes of smoke. Even the bread. | We eat our supper at the furnace. Warm as you like. | The soot gets in the butter.
b : It's like seasoning. | Warm's something. | Scrape it off.
a : Seasoning. Yes. | It's all something. | I scrape it off. Then there's no butter.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- The towns' own food

scene e1_food_fens_eels
when topic=food here.region=fens
a : Eel pie on Restday. Like my father, and his. | The eel traps were full this morning. | Smoked eel, jellied eel, eel in a pie. That's the Fens for you.
b[from=mirebridge] : Nothing better in the realm. I'll hear no word against it.
b[from!=mirebridge] : I still can't look an eel in the eye.
b : Long may it last. | Lucky you. | And eel in a stew.
a : Long may it. | I'll bring you one. | Eel in everything.

scene e1_food_fens_reeds
when topic=food here.region=fens here.season=spring|summer
a : Duck eggs from the reed beds. Big as your paw. | The wildfowl are thick on the water this year. | Watercress from the ditches. Free, if you don't mind wet feet.
b[child] : I fell in getting eggs. Mother didn't speak to me all day.
b : Good eating. | Pot one for me. | Wet feet's the Fens anyway.
a : The best. | If my sling's kind. | Always wet feet.

scene e1_food_frost_smoked
when topic=food here.region=frosthollow
a : Smoked fish and black bread. That's a Frosthollow winter. | My grandmother smoked fish in the chimney. The whole house tasted of it. | Nothing grows up here. We eat what keeps.
b[from=frosthollow] : And a dram after. Don't forget the dram.
b[from!=frosthollow] : I'd kill for something green.
b : Keeps you alive. | Did you mind? | And what keeps is good.
a : And keeps you grumpy. | Loved it. | Mostly.

scene e1_food_frost_cold_feast
when topic=food here.region=frosthollow here.season=winter
a : The snow's too deep for the carts. We're down to oats. | Mutton fat and oats. You'll live through anything on that. | The stores are getting low and the pass is shut.
b[miner] : The mine eats first. Always has.
b : Oats it is. | You'll live. You won't enjoy it. | We'll see spring.
a : Oats and patience. | I don't enjoy it. | We always have.

scene e1_food_coldwater_trout
when topic=food here.region=coldwater
a : Lake trout, fried in butter. I could weep. | They're bringing the char in by the boatload. | The water's so clear you can pick your supper from the jetty.
b[fisher] : Clear water, clever fish. They see you coming.
b : Weep, then. I'll eat it. | Good year for it. | Show me.
a : Get your own. | Fat ones. | Bring a net.

scene e1_food_oakhaven_mushrooms
when topic=food here.region=woods
a : The woods are full of ceps after that rain. | Venison's cheap this month. The hunters had a good run. | Found a ring of mushrooms by the old stump. Big as plates.
b[from=oakhaven] : Show me where. No. Don't tell anyone else.
b[healer] : Bring them to me first. Big as plates is how they kill you.
b : I'll fry some for us both. | Get it while you can. | Which stump?
a : Butter and salt. | Every year it's the same. | That's my secret.

scene e1_food_oakhaven_boar
when topic=food here.region=woods here.season=autumn|winter
a : They brought a boar in from the deep wood. Whole village is eating. | Acorn-fed pork. Best you'll ever taste. | Rabbit pie and blackberries. Oakhaven in autumn.
b[woodcutter] : I saw that boar. It nearly ate me first.
b : Save me a rib. | I don't doubt it. | Sounds like a song.
a : If there's any left. | You will. | It ought to be.

scene e1_food_larkfield_mutton
when topic=food here.region=larkfield
a : Larkfield mutton. They talk about it in Ridgemere, you know. | Mutton and turnip. Mutton and barley. Mutton and mutton. | Too many sheep, not enough mint sauce.
b[herder] : Best grazing in the realm. That's what you're tasting.
b[from=larkfield] : Proper mutton's a year old at least. Lamb's for babies.
b : They should. | Mutton and more mutton. | There's never enough mint.
a : Let them. | I'll not complain. | Never enough.

scene e1_food_larkfield_shearing
when topic=food here.region=larkfield here.season=spring|summer
a : Shearing supper's on Fiveday. Cold meat and cider for everyone. | I've baked forty loaves for the shearers. | They eat like wolves, shearers. Well. We're all wolves.
b[farmer] : Earn it with a pair of shears first.
b : I'll be there. | Forty! | Hungrier wolves, then.
a : Bring a mug. | Forty-one, counting mine. | Much hungrier.

scene e1_food_southmeadow_fruit
when topic=food here.region=southmeadow
a : Peaches. Off the tree, still warm. Nothing in the realm like it. | The plums are in. You can smell them from the road. | They say up north they've never seen a fig.
b[from=southmeadow] : I went to Ridgemere once. Their fruit's a crime. Small and hard.
b[from!=southmeadow] : I'll be fat as a vicar by the time I leave.
b : Spoils you for anywhere else. | A good year. | Pity them.
a : It does. | The best. | I do.

scene e1_food_southmeadow_wine
when topic=food here.region=southmeadow here.season=autumn
a : The grapes are in. Everyone with feet is treading. | Last year's wine is ready. Better than the year before. | You've purple paws.
b[child] : I trod grapes! My feet are purple! Look!
b : I'll lend a foot. | Better every year. | So would you.
a : Wash it first. | It's the sun. | I won't deny it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Homesick for home cooking

scene e1_food_homesick_eels
when topic=food a.from=mirebridge here.region!=fens
a : Nobody here can make an eel pie. Nobody. | I'd give my coat for a plate of Fen eels. | They look at me like I'm mad when I ask for eels.
b[from=mirebridge] : Same as me. I've a cousin who sends them, smoked.
b : I've never had one. | You'd freeze. | You might be.
a : Your loss. | It'd be worth it. | Mad for eels.

scene e1_food_homesick_south
when topic=food a.from=southmeadow here.region!=southmeadow
a : The apples up here taste of nothing. | I miss real wine. This is vinegar with ideas. | My mother's peach jam. I'd cry if I saw a jar.
b[from=ridgemere] : Our apples are fine. You're spoiled.
b : They taste of apple. | Drink it anyway. | Write to her.
a : Of water, then. | I do. Every night. | I have. Twice.

scene e1_food_homesick_north
when topic=food a.from=frosthollow|coldwater here.region=south
a : Too warm for smoked fish down here. I've tried. | I'd swap all this fruit for a bit of black bread. | Nobody makes a broth strong enough here.
b[from=southmeadow] : Black bread? On purpose?
b : Too warm for anything. | Truly? | Ours is fine.
a : Too warm for me, certainly. | Truly. | It's soup water.

scene e1_food_homesick_larkfield
when topic=food a.from=larkfield here.region!=larkfield
a : Their mutton here's goat. I'd swear to it. | I miss a proper Larkfield pie. | Nobody here knows what to do with a sheep.
b[from=larkfield] : Our grandmothers would weep.
b : It's mutton. | What's in it? | Shear it.
a : It's goat. | Mutton, pastry, more mutton. | Very funny.

scene e1_food_newcomer_dish
when topic=food pair.same_from=no
a : What do they eat where you're from? | Is it true your lot put fruit in with the meat? | I tried your country's dish. Once.
b[from=mirebridge] : Eels. Mainly eels.
b[from=larkfield] : Mutton. More mutton. Then mutton.
b[from=southmeadow] : Fruit, wine, bread. And everything else with fruit in.
b[from=frosthollow] : Whatever keeps. Smoked, salted, dried.
b : Same as here, more or less. | Only sometimes. | And?
a : Huh. | Sometimes's enough. | Once.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Friends, family, spouses

scene e1_food_spouses_cooking
when topic=food pair.band=spouses
a : Who's cooking tonight? | You put too much salt in, last night. | I'll make that soup you like.
b : You. I did yesterday. | I put the right amount. You're wrong. | Which one? I like all your soups.
a : You did not. | I'm never wrong about salt. | The green one.
b?[humour=merry] : I'll eat anything you make, #dear#. Even the green one.

scene e1_food_friends_pie
when topic=food pair.band=friends
a : I'll bring you a pie on Restday. | Remember the night we ate a whole cheese between us? | You still owe me supper.
b : I'll hold you to it. | My belly remembers. | I owe you nothing.
a : You should. | Never again. Once more. | One supper. I've a long memory.

scene e1_food_family_recipe
when topic=food pair.band=family
a : Mother's recipe's wrong. You left out the bay. | Who has Granny's pie dish? | You always got the bigger plate.
b : I left it out on purpose. | I do. And I'm keeping it. | I was bigger.
a : There's no purpose to it. | We'll see. | You're still bigger.

scene e1_food_lodger_supper
when topic=food pair.kin=household
a : Whose turn to cook? | Somebody ate my cheese. | Did you finish the bread?
b : Yours. Definitely yours. | It wasn't me. | Define finish.
a : It's never mine. | You've crumbs on your muzzle. | There's one crust left.
b : It's always yours. | Mice. Big mice. | Then I didn't finish it.

scene e1_food_neighbours_smell
when topic=food pair.neighbours=yes
a : Your cooking comes through my wall every evening. | What were you frying last night? Smelt wonderful. | Your kitchen smoke goes straight in my window.
b : Sorry. Or you're welcome. | Bacon. Don't tell my healer. | Shut the window.
a : Both. | I'll tell anyone who asks. | I'd stifle.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Oddities and stories

scene e1_food_cake_disaster
when topic=food
a : I made a cake for my mother. It fell in the middle. | My pie came out of the oven like a boot. | I tried to make bread. It's more of a brick.
b[baker] : Too much yeast, or a cold kitchen. One of the two.
b : Fill it with cream. Nobody'll know. | Boots are useful. | Bricks are useful too.
a : She'll know. She always knows. | Not to eat. | Not to eat.

scene e1_food_wedding_food
when topic=food world.wedding=recent
a : The food at that wedding! I'm still full. | They had a whole roasted pig at the wedding. | I took a pie home from the wedding in my hat.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : I wasn't asked. I could smell it, though.
b : Generous family. | A whole one! | In your hat?
a : They'll be eating cheap for a year. | Head and all. | My good hat.

scene e1_food_funeral_ham
when topic=food world.death=recent
a : There's always ham at a funeral. Why ham? | They put on a good spread for the burying. | Grief makes me hungry. Is that wrong?
b[old] : Ham keeps. Grief doesn't let you cook.
b : Ham's respectful. | They did. | No. Grief's hungry work.
a : Ham's sorrowful. | The least they could do. | Pass the ham, then.

scene e1_food_prices_bread
when topic=food world.meal_price=up
a : A loaf's gone up again. | I bought less and paid more. How does that happen? | I can't afford the stew I used to sniff at.
b[baker] : Flour's gone up. I'm not getting rich, believe me.
b[wealth=rich] : Has it? I hadn't noticed.
b : Everything's gone up. | Magic. | We'll all be on turnips.
a : Except wages. | Dark magic. | I'm on turnips.

scene e1_food_prices_cheap
when topic=food world.meal_price=down
a : Bread's cheaper this week. Don't say it too loud. | I bought two pies for the price of one. | Somebody's had a good harvest. The market's full.
b[merchant] : It won't last. Buy now.
b : Shh. | Lucky. | Long may it last.
a : I said it quietly. | Ate both. | It won't.

scene e1_food_moonlight_snack
when topic=food here.time=night
a : I'm raiding the larder when I get home. | Cold sausage at midnight. That's living. | Nothing tastes as good as stolen food in the dark.
b[young] : My mother locks the larder. I know where the key is.
b : Quietly, I hope. | That's sin. | Your own larder's not stealing.
a : Like a shadow. | Delicious sin. | It feels like it.
